@using Umbraco.Cms.Core.Models.Blocks
@inherits Umbraco.Cms.Web.Common.Views.UmbracoViewPage<IEnumerable<BlockGridItem>>
@{
    if (Model?.Any() != true) { return; }
}

<div class="umb-block-grid__layout-container">
    @foreach (var item in Model)
    {

        <div
            class="umb-block-grid__layout-item"
            data-content-element-type-alias="@item.Content.ContentType.Alias"
            data-content-element-type-key="@item.Content.ContentType.Key"
            data-element-key="@item.ContentKey"
            data-col-span="@item.ColumnSpan"
            data-row-span="@item.RowSpan"
            style=" --umb-block-grid--item-column-span: @item.ColumnSpan; --umb-block-grid--item-row-span: @item.RowSpan; ">
            @{
                var partialViewName = "blockgrid/Components/" + item.Content.ContentType.Alias;
                try
                {
                    @await Html.PartialAsync(partialViewName, item)
                }
                catch (InvalidOperationException)
                {
                    <p>
                        <strong>Could not render component of type: @(item.Content.ContentType.Alias)</strong>
                        <br/>
                        This likely happened because the partial view <em>@partialViewName</em> could not be found.
                    </p>
                }
            }
        </div>
    }
</div>

content.thinking:
Key findings:
- `_ViewImports.cshtml` already imports Umbraco.Extensions, PublishedModels, Web.Common.Views, Core.Models.PublishedContent, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Html, and tag helpers. So `GetBlockGridHtmlAsync`, `GetCropUrl`, `Children<T>()`, `Url()` friendly extensions are available (Umbraco.Extensions).
- `items.cshtml` looks up `"blockgrid/Components/" + item.Content.ContentType.Alias` — confirms adapter path `Views/Partials/blockgrid/Components/<alias>.cshtml`.
- Tailwind v4 (@tailwindcss/vite ^4.0.0, tailwindcss ^4.0.0). Tailwind v4 supports arbitrary variants `[&_p]:mb-4`. In v4, `@import "tailwindcss"` includes preflight. Note: Tailwind v4 preflight — paragraphs have margin 0. I'll use arbitrary variants for spacing.
- Models/ contains only ModelsBuilder/ — I need to create Models/Blocks/.
- Project folders: Composers/, Migrations/, Models/, Notifications/, Program.cs, Services/, Views/. No Components/ folder. Where do ViewComponents go? AGENTS.md conventions: "One composer per concern in Composers/; services in Services/; notification handlers in Notifications/; migrations in Migrations/". It doesn't say where ViewComponents go. Common Umbraco scaffold convention: a `Components/` folder at project root for ViewComponents (that's the classic Umbraco convention — `Components/HeroViewComponent.cs`). Since AGENTS.md doesn't forbid it and it's the Umbraco convention, I'll create `Components/` at the Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco project root with the three ViewComponent classes. Namespace: `Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Components`.

Hmm wait — will ViewComponents be discovered automatically? ASP.NET Core discovers ViewComponent classes by convention: any public class ending in "ViewComponent" (or implementing IViewComponent) in the application assembly is discovered automatically. Yes — no registration needed. And `Component.InvokeAsync("RichText", ...)` resolves by name (class name minus "ViewComponent" suffix).

- site.css scans `../../Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco/Views` via @source — so Tailwind classes in my Razor views get picked up. 

Now, AGENTS.md says for the page views: "Each starts with @using Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models and @inherits UmbracoViewPage<BlogFrontPage> (or <BlogPost>), sets Layout = null, and is a whole HTML page (doctype, head with a title and <link rel=stylesheet href=~/dist/site.css asp-append-version=true>, body). There is no shared layout in this scaffold."

For the adapters: "One adapter per block in Views/Partials/blockgrid/Components/<alias>.cshtml, starting with @using Umbraco.Cms.Core.Models.Blocks, @using Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models and @inherits UmbracoViewPage<BlockGridItem<RichTextBlock>> (the block's class). It maps Model.Content to a plain model in Models/Blocks/ and calls @await Component.InvokeAsync with it; no markup."

For components: "One ViewComponent per block with its view in Views/Shared/Components/<Name>/Default.cshtml. Rich text: the adapter passes Model.Content.Text?.ToHtmlString() as a string, and the view prints it with @Html.Raw(Model.Html), or the tags show up as text."

Also from the pipeline section: "maps the ModelsBuilder content (and settings: spacing, background, anchor) to a plain view model" — our blocks have no settings element type, so just content.

Plan of files:

1. `Models/Blocks/RichTextModel.cs`:
```csharp
namespace Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models.Blocks;

public sealed class RichTextModel
{
    public required string Html { get; init; }
}
```
Hmm — "Write code that reads like the surrounding code: match its comment density, naming, and idiom." The scaffold's generated models use file-scoped? No — generated uses block namespace. For hand-written, the scaffold's Composers/Services — let me check one to match idiom (file-scoped namespaces vs block, sealed classes, etc.). Let me read a Service and a Composer quickly.

2. `Models/Blocks/ImageModel.cs`:
```csharp
public sealed class ImageModel
{
    public string? ImageUrl { get; init; }
    public string? Caption { get; init; }
}
```

3. `Models/Blocks/QuoteModel.cs`:
```csharp
public sealed class QuoteModel
{
    public string? Quote { get; init; }
    public string? Attribution { get; init; }
}
```

4. `Components/RichTextViewComponent.cs`:
```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models.Blocks;

namespace Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Components;

public sealed class RichTextViewComponent : ViewComponent
{
    public IViewComponentResult Invoke(RichTextModel model) => View(model);
}
```

Wait — AGENTS.md example:
```csharp
public sealed class HeroViewComponent : ViewComponent
{
    public IViewComponentResult Invoke(HeroModel model) => View(model);
}
```
Match that exactly.

5. `Components/ImageViewComponent.cs` — Invoke(ImageModel model) => View(model);
6. `Components/QuoteViewComponent.cs` — Invoke(QuoteModel model) => View(model);

7. `Views/Partials/blockgrid/Components/richTextBlock.cshtml`:
```cshtml
@using Umbraco.Cms.Core.Models.Blocks
@using Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models
@using Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models.Blocks
@inherits UmbracoViewPage<BlockGridItem<RichTextBlock>>
@{
    var model = new RichTextModel
    {
        Html = Model.Content.Text?.ToHtmlString() ?? "",
    };
}
