Now — one subtle thing about the `new { model }` anonymous object and component Invoke parameter binding: `Component.InvokeAsync("RichText", new { model })` — the ViewComponent's Invoke(RichTextModel model) — ViewComponent argument binding binds anonymous object properties to parameters by name. `model` matches. Good.

Also — hmm, `Component.InvokeAsync("Image", ...)` — wait, is there any chance "Image" collides with a tag helper or something? No, InvokeAsync takes a component name string. Fine.

Let me also double check `GetBlockGridHtmlAsync(Model, "body")` — the friendly HTML helper extension: `GetBlockGridHtmlAsync(this IHtmlHelper htmlHelper, IPublishedContent content, string alias, ...)` — in Umbraco.Extensions (Umbraco.Cms.Web.Common.HtmlHelperExtensions? The friendly block grid extensions are in `Umbraco.Extensions` namespace, class `FriendlyHtmlHelperExtensions`? hmm — AGENTS.md says `@await Html.GetBlockGridHtmlAsync(Model, "blocks")` — trust it; _ViewImports has Umbraco.Extensions).

OK — also the front page `Model.Children<BlogPost>()` — friendly `Children<T>(this IPublishedContent content, ...)` in Umbraco.Extensions. Good.

Now write all 11 files. Batch them — all independent. That's 3 models + 3 components + 3 adapters + 3 component views + 2 page templates = 14 files. I can do them in a few messages. Let me do models+components (6) in one batch, then adapters+component views (6) in another, then the 2 page templates. Actually all 14 are independent — but that's a lot of tool calls in one message; it's fine, they're all Write calls. Let me split into two messages for manageability: first 6 (C# files), then 8 (cshtml files).

